RAP Discharge Date Simulation — Detailed Technical Documentation
1. Background & Problem Context
RAP (Readmission Avoidance Program) begins scoring members immediately once an inpatient (IP) authorization is received.

Because authorization is often submitted at admission, members frequently get identified **before the actual discharge date**.

 

Care Management (CM) teams reported operational issues:

• Members are very hard to reach while inpatient → low UTR, low engagement  

• Nurses manually check discharge status → productivity loss  

• Too many “early” cases; too few “timely post‑discharge” opportunities  

 

Analytics was asked to:

1. Measure delays in receiving discharge date in auth system  

2. Estimate impact of using predicted discharge date as proxy  

3. Evaluate operational impact of delaying identification closer to discharge

2. Data Used
Primary date fields:

• first_scored_dt — First RAP scoring date  

• actual_discharge_dt — Actual discharge  

• actual_discharge_loaded_dt — When discharge arrives in auth system  

• admit_dt — Inpatient admit date  

• tum_act_los_day_cnt — True LOS  

 

Prediction model fields:

• avg_los_grp, median_los_grp  

• avg_los_ctg, median_los_ctg  

 

We used 2024 for model logic; 2025 data for evaluation.

3. LOS Prediction Modeling Summary
Several rule-based LOS models were attempted:

1. Median LOS by diagnosis category  

2. Median LOS by diagnosis + service type + admission status  

 

Performance:

• Baseline MAE ≈ 2.41 days  

• Enhanced grouping MAE ≈ 2.20 days  

 

This accuracy is sufficient to approximate a “predicted discharge date.”

4. Current Scoring Behavior (Baseline)
Lag calculation:

lag_current = first_scored_dt − actual_discharge_dt

 

Results:

• 69% of cases identified BEFORE discharge  

• 28% identified within RAP 0–4 day window  

• 2% late (≥5 days post‑discharge)

 

Operational issue: Scoring too early → low member engagement.

5. Predicted Discharge Simulation Logic
Simulated scoring date:

sim_score_dt = earliest(

   predicted_discharge_dt,

   actual_discharge_loaded_dt,

   first_scored_dt

)

 

This ensures:

• We do not score before actual discharge if discharge posts earlier  

• We do not score later than current process  

• Predicted discharge anchors scoring near day 0

6. Bucket Definitions
EARLY (before discharge):

• Early_on_or_before_admit → sim_score_dt ≤ admit_dt  

• Early_>5_before_DC → lag ≤ −5  

• Early_4_5_before_DC → lag = −4 or −5  

• Early_1_3_before_DC → lag = −1 to −3  

 

GOOD (RAP opportunity):

• On_discharge_date → lag = 0  

• Timely_0_4_days → lag = 1 to 4  

 

LATE (lost opportunity):

• Late_5_7  

• Late_8_10  

• Late_>10

7. Simulation Results (Shift −5 to +5)
We tested the impact of shifting predicted discharge date by:

shift ∈ {−5, −4, …, 0, …, +5}

 

Metrics evaluated:

• capture_rate (0–4 days)  

• early_frac  

• good_0_4d_frac  

• late_frac  

 

Shift = 0 results:

• Early reduced from 69% → 41%  

• Good/timely increased from 28% → 57%  

• Late remained low around 2–3%  

 

This is the “sweet spot.”

8. Key Findings
1. Predicted discharge date dramatically reduces early identification load  

2. RAP capture rate remains stable around 97–98%  

3. Good identification nearly doubles  

4. Very small increase in late cases  

5. Excellent operational improvement with minimal risk

9. Histogram + Cumulative % Insights
Two plots were produced:

1. Current scoring vs discharge  

2. Predicted scoring vs discharge  

 

Observations:

• Current scoring distribution heavily left-skewed (inpatient scoring)  

• Predicted scoring centered around day 0  

• Cumulative % reaches accurate 97% by day 4  

• Visual shift confirms reduction in early identification

10. Recommended Implementation
Final scoring logic should be:

final_score_dt = earliest(

   predicted_discharge_dt,

   actual_discharge_loaded_dt,

   first_scored_dt

)

 

Expected CM improvements:

• Higher engagement  

• Less manual discharge checking  

• More timely post-discharge outreach  

• Better member experience

11. Next Steps
• Validate on more 2025 months (Q1–Q3)  

• Validate performance by LOB: Medicare / Medicaid / Commercial / DSNP  

• Evaluate facility-level behavior  

• Consider additional signals (admit class, attending specialty)  

• Define production logic & monitoring